---
import ConverterPage from '../components/ConverterPage.astro';
---

<ConverterPage
  path="/about"
  lede="Why MyKrutiDev exists and how it works."
  faq={[]}
>
  <h2>What this is</h2>
  <p>
    A Hindi legacy-font converter that tries to get the hard parts right: the
    short-i matra that sits on the wrong side of its consonant, the reph that is
    typed after the syllable it belongs to, the vowel signs that legacy fonts
    draw as two separate glyphs, and the conjuncts
    <strong lang="hi">क्ष</strong>, <strong lang="hi">त्र</strong>,
    <strong lang="hi">ज्ञ</strong> and <strong lang="hi">श्र</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>Your text stays on your machine</h2>
  <p>
    There is no server. The conversion engine is JavaScript that runs in your
    browser, so nothing you paste is uploaded, logged or stored. You can load
    the page, disconnect from the internet, and it will still work. For anyone
    handling draft orders, file notings or personal records, that is the point.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it is incomplete</h2>
  <p>
    Kruti Dev and Unicode are done and covered by tests. DevLys is seeded from
    the Kruti Dev layout and mostly works but has not been verified. Chanakya is
    not mapped at all yet, and the converter refuses that pair rather than
    guessing. Pages that are not finished are marked and kept out of search
    results.
  </p>
  <p>
    English-word detection is a heuristic, not a certainty. A legacy font
    stores Hindi as plain ASCII, so there is no way to be perfect about it.
    Numbers, dates, URLs and email addresses are always safe.
  </p>
</ConverterPage>
